<h2>The suit and its long valuation battle</h2>
<p>The appellant sued her brother and sister for partition, possession, rendition of accounts and permanent injunction over properties in Delhi and Rishikesh, together with business income and cash, claiming they were their father&rsquo;s self-acquired assets that devolved equally on intestacy. The brother&rsquo;s answer was structural: both parents had left wills in his favour, several properties had moved to him under registered sale and gift deeds, and the suit was undervalued, improperly stamped and time-barred.</p>
<p>What followed, over several years, was a procedural war of attrition. The trial court repeatedly directed the plaintiff to value each relief separately and pay ad valorem court fees. She instead sought amendments, which were rejected — rejections the High Court itself upheld. Ultimately the District Judge rejected the plaint under Order VII Rule 11(b) and (d) CPC for non-payment of court fees and limitation, and the plaintiff appealed.</p>

<h2>The appellate analysis</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Possession test</strong> — the plaint itself showed the plaintiff was not in possession of any suit property, so ad valorem fees were required on each relief.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Opportunities exhausted</strong> — the plaintiff failed to value the reliefs separately and pay the fee despite several chances; rejection under clause (b) was therefore justified.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Title obstacle</strong> — a partition suit could not proceed while the wills standing in the way of the claimed title were deliberately left unchallenged.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Limitation</strong> — the plaintiff knew of the wills and deeds; the period for a declaratory challenge had long expired, attracting clause (d).</div>
</div>

<h2>The discipline of Order VII Rule 11</h2>
<p>The Court acknowledged that the power to reject a plaint is &ldquo;drastic in consequence and must be exercised with circumspection.&rdquo; But circumspection is not indulgence. Where the defect is one of valuation and fee, the statute itself builds in the cure: the court fixes a time, and rejection follows only if the plaintiff does not comply. A plaintiff who treats those orders as negotiable — seeking amendment after amendment rather than paying — ultimately elects the consequence.</p>

<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><h3>How the plaint was framed</h3><p>A co-heir&rsquo;s partition claim on intestacy, nominally valued, with the wills, sale deeds and gift deeds left unaddressed.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><h3>What the facts required</h3><p>A suit by an out-of-possession claimant seeking possession and declarations against registered instruments — ad valorem fees, specific challenges, and all of it within limitation.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Lessons for property litigants</h2>
<p>Three practical rules emerge. First, plead possession honestly and value accordingly: the court fee question is decided on the plaint&rsquo;s own averments, and an artful silence about dispossession will not survive scrutiny. Second, attack the obstacle documents: wills, sale deeds and gift deeds that block the claimed title must be specifically challenged, within limitation, with the ad valorem consequences that follow. Third, treat valuation orders as deadlines, not openings for renegotiation — the right to cure is finite, and its exhaustion converts a curable defect into a terminal one.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>An order rejecting a plaint does not, in principle, bar a fresh suit on a properly framed and funded plaint — but limitation keeps running, and in a case like this, where the declaratory challenge was already time-barred, the rejection is as good as final. The time to get the architecture of a partition suit right is before filing, not after the third costs order.</p>
</div>
